grade 3 teacher Christy chippenack has  been living with terrible tastes and  smells for nearly a year there are very  few Foods she can tolerate lettuce and  tomatoes are still my Staples mostly  other things either taste like dirt or  just tastes like they've already gone  bad to Christy most meat tastes rotten  coffee smells like gas her disorder is  called parasmia it showed up shortly  after she had covid-19 when Christy  shared her story with global news last  may she hoped relief would come soon it  didn't instead she's learning to cope  and making videos about it I don't add  banana anymore because they taste like  chemicals unfortunately Quebec  researcher Johannes fresnelli says smell  and taste issues still affect many  infected with covid we know now it  depends a little bit about on on the  variants that we're looking at but  approximately 60 percent of of infected  people lose their sense of smell in the  acute phase in his study of health care  workers how who lost their smell said it  was not back to normal a year later the  others that they smell they don't smell  as they are supposed to smell so instead  of vanilla they would smell let's say  burnt rubber or something like that  Edmonton's long covid Clinic continues  to see patients with parasmia and  anosmia or no smell the most common  treatment is olfactory training several  times a day they just open up the vial  and they practice smelling kind of like  resetting the sensors and the uh and the  part of the brain that um that uh  perceives smell Christie says the only  thing that's helped her so far is  connecting with others with porosmia  they tell me like oh my gosh I thought I  was the only one and it does feel like  that and it can be very isolating online  support groups share tips and recipes  Bland Cuisine Christy hopes she won't  have to eat for much longer I cannot  wait I have a whole list of things I'll  be having that day let me tell you sue  lingo Global News
 

